      "problem": "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ 0-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, there are no $y$-intercepts.",
